 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Memory Constraints:** Standard feed-forward methods fail to train at 2K or higher resolutions due to memory constraints (OOM). LGTM trains successfully at 2K and 4K using under 30 GB of memory [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Holes in Rendering:** Without high-resolution supervision, predicted Gaussians may result in areas with holes as they are not anti-aliased. High-resolution supervision forces the network to learn proper coverage [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Geometric Stability:** In Stage 2, the primitive network must be trained with a reduced learning rate (0.1x) to maintain geometric stability while the texture network learns appearance details [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Constraints:** Standard feed-forward methods fail at 2K+ resolutions due to memory (e.g., NoPoSplat requires >61GB at 1024x576). LGTM uses <30GB at 4K [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Geometric Stability:** In Stage 2, the primitive network must be trained with a reduced learning rate (0.1x) to prevent geometric collapse while learning textures [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Texture Initialization:** Color texture $T^i_c$ is zero-initialized to ensure the SH base color dominates initially, preventing high-frequency noise [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Anti-aliasing:** High-resolution supervision is critical in Stage 1; without it, predicted Gaussians may result in holes when rendered at high resolution [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Memory Usage:** LGTM trains successfully at 2K and 4K resolutions using under 30 GB of memory. In contrast, NoPoSplat fails to train at 2K or higher due to memory constraints (OOM), reaching 61.85 GB for a batch size of 1 at 1024x576 primitives [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Scalability:** LGTM scales efficiently at inference; a 64x pixel increase adds only modest memory and time overhead [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Enables high-fidelity 4K novel view synthesis, a capability previously out of reach for feed-forward methods [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Decouples geometry and appearance, allowing compact primitives to represent rich textures. Feed-forward, no per-scene optimization required. Versatile, operating with or without known camera poses (monocular, posed two-view, unposed two-view, multi-view) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires a dual-network architecture and staged training. Texture resolution $T$ is a tunable parameter that trades off primitive size and texture size [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time and Memory Complexity:** LGTM scales efficiently at inference; a 64x pixel increase adds only modest memory and time overhead [S1, unofficial]. It trains successfully at 2K and 4K resolutions using under 30 GB of memory [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** LGTM enables high-fidelity 4K novel view synthesis, a capability previously out of reach for feed-forward methods [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Decouples geometry and appearance; enables high-resolution rendering with significantly fewer primitives; no per-scene optimization required; versatile (works with monocular, posed/unposed two-view, and multi-view inputs) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires a dual-network architecture; relies on the quality of the baseline method (Flash3D, DepthSplat, etc.) for initial geometry prediction [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When high-resolution (4K) novel view synthesis is required without per-scene optimization, and computational resources are limited (<30GB VRAM).
+*   **When not to use:** When per-scene optimization is acceptable and lower resolution is sufficient, or when input does not include high-resolution images for the texture network [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-LGTM was published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026 by Yixing Lao, Xuyang Bai, Xiaoyang Wu, Nuoyuan Yan, Zixin Luo, Jean-Daniel Nahmias, Yanghai Tsin, Shiwei Li, and Hengshuang Zhao from HKU and Apple [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitations of existing feed-forward 3D Gaussian Splatting methods, which suffer from quadratic growth in primitive count as resolution increases, and textured Gaussian methods that require per-scene optimization [S1, unofficial].
+LGTM was published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026 by Yixing Lao, Xuyang Bai, Xiaoyang Wu, Nuoyuan Yan, Zixin Luo, Jean-Daniel Nahmias, Yanghai Tsin, Shiwei Li, and Hengshuang Zhao from HKU and Apple [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitations of prior textured Gaussian methods that require per-scene optimization and the resolution scalability bottleneck of feed-forward methods [S1, unofficial].
 
